{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kJCoFYRK25oLrF71Lf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three rows changed meaning, and I blessed and re-anchored all three. Two commits, each ending `WI: WI-923`, and the commit checks passed on both:\n\n| Commit | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `244ba8e45` | The verdict under `## amendment`: `VERDICT: MEANING rows=3`, then `SITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment`. The kit's parser accepts it under the combined binding. |\n| `25ef8e255` | `snapshot --reattests LLR-270,TC-267,TC-329`. No other drifted row blocked the copy. |\n\n**Why meaning:** LLR-270 now says how the store lock handles failures creating its lock file:\n- **Access denied:** read as \"lock held\" and retried until the wait runs out. On Windows that refusal happens while another holder is still deleting the lock.\n- **Still held at the deadline:** refused as busy, naming the lock and, when the last attempt met one, the access refusal.\n- **Any other refusal:** raised on the first attempt.\n\nCode written to the old text could fail on the first access-denied attempt, or report \"busy\" without saying why, and so fail the new text.\n\n**Why blessed:**\n- **Parent:** SR-227's store is written whole by one holder at a time. That needs a lock that doesn't mistake a holder mid-delete for a hard failure, and that says why it gave up.\n- **Closed and named only once:** the rule covers three cases, each with a fixed outcome, and it names nothing the approved cell didn't already name.\n- **Code match:** it matches `session_keep.dir_lock` and `_try_lock`.\n- **Tests:** TC-267 adds one test per case, and TC-329 adds the case where a racing first call meets an access-denied lock. It runs without retention and leaves the first call's lease alone. All four new tests exist and no evidence was removed.\n\nThe untracked `004-ADJUDICATE-238c2b1.md.requested` belongs to the coordinator, and I didn't touch it."}],"contai